# Citation metadata (#15). GitHub shows a "Cite this repository" button from this file, and Zenodo
# reads it for the DOI record of each release. Keep `version` in step with pyproject.toml.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: "COAST: Corridor-Optimized Aerially-Safe Trajectories"
abstract: >-
  COAST is a real-time minimum-snap B-spline trajectory planner for multirotors flying through
  cluttered, partially-unknown environments. An A* front end builds Safe Flight Corridors, and
  a quadratic-programming back end (OSQP) optimises snap-minimised control points inside them
  with MINVO kinodynamic bounds. C++17 engine with Python bindings (coast-planner on PyPI).
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Alejandro
    family-names: Pelaez
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-0848-4365"
    affiliation: "MAGICC Lab, Brigham Young University"
  - given-names: Brady
    family-names: Moon
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4297-3938"
    affiliation: "FROST Lab, Brigham Young University"
  - given-names: Randal
    family-names: Beard
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-2548-5329"
    affiliation: "MAGICC Lab, Brigham Young University"
license: BSD-3-Clause
repository-code: "https://github.com/byu-magicc/Minimum-Snap-Trajectories"
version: 0.1.0
keywords:
  - trajectory optimization
  - minimum snap
  - B-splines
  - safe flight corridors
  - quadrotors
  - motion planning
